Since the development of the Fourier-transform deconvolution procedure for OHD-RIKES data by McMorrow and Lotshaw (22), the intermolecular dynamics of a wide range of liquids have been studied with this technique (26,52,65–85). Figure 13 illustrates representative OKE reduced spectral densities we have recorded in symmetric-top liquids, including acetonitrile, benzene, benzene-d , carbon disulfide, chloroform, hexafluorobenzene, mesitylene, and 1,3,5-trifluorobenzene. Although there are conspicuous differences among these spectra, they are all broad and relatively featureless. Indeed, with rare exceptions the reduced spectral densities of simple liquids are devoid of sharp features, which makes it difficult to find an unambiguous interpretation of these spectra. text
